# dfa.topic -- declared finite-state recognizers (`class`, `dfa`), their
# protocol (std::dfa) and the diagnostics of their declarations.

@topic concept.recognizers
title: Declared recognizers: `dfa` and `class`
summary: `dfa NAME over ALPHABET:` declares a finite-state recognizer of one token, checked by the compiler and run as an ordinary coroutine that returns how many symbols it leaves unread.
index: concepts
alias: dfa; recognizer; finite automaton; lexer; tokenizer; class declaration; accept before; lookahead
keywords: dfa, class, recognizer, automaton, DFA, lexer, tokenizer, token, accept, accept before, lookahead, unread, end, call, then, over, bytes, ascii, unicode, sequence, std::dfa, UnrecognizedInput, UnexpectedEnd
related: concept.coroutines stdlib.module.std::dfa error.dfa-ambiguous-transition error.dfa-unread-beyond-input error.dfa-call-lookahead
docs: DFA-COROUTINE-MILESTONE-2.md DFA-COROUTINE-EXPLORATION.md COROUTINES.md COROUTINE-FUSION-MILESTONE-1.md

== WHAT IT IS
A recognizer is a table: states, and for each state the symbols it takes
and what it does with them. The compiler checks the table and lowers it to
an ordinary coroutine function of the protocol of std::dfa:

    fn NAME(first: int, resume std::dfa::Symbol) -> int
            errors std::dfa::UnrecognizedInput, std::dfa::UnexpectedEnd

`coroutine {step, first} = NAME(c)` starts it on the token's first
symbol; `step(std::dfa::Symbol {code: c})` hands it the next. While it runs,
each yield answers 0 ("taken, send the next"); its completion
(`std::coroutine::done?(step)`) returns how many of the symbols it was
handed are not part of its token. The end of the input is the code -1,
never a symbol, and never counted.

    class digit = '0'..'9'

    dfa number over ascii:
        start:
            '-' -> minus
            digit -> integer
        minus:
            digit -> integer
        integer:
            digit -> integer
            ' ' ',' -> accept before
            end -> accept

== THE DECLARATION
  * `class NAME = SYMBOLS [except SYMBOLS]`: a named symbol set, at the top
    level (a module's classes are named `ns::NAME` after `import ns`) or
    first in a dfa. A symbol is a character literal, a decimal or `0x`
    hexadecimal integer, a range `'a'..'z'`, a class, or `any` (the
    alphabet of the recognizer that reads it).
  * `dfa NAME over bytes|ascii|unicode:`: bytes are 0..255, ascii 0..127
    (the same symbols in every alphabet), unicode the Unicode scalar
    values. The first state starts.
  * A transition is `SYMBOLS -> ACTION`: a state (take the symbol and
    continue there), `accept` (complete, the symbol part of the token),
    `accept before N` (complete, the last N symbols handed over not part of
    it; `accept before` is N = 1), `call DFA then STATE` or `call DFA then
    accept` (hand the symbol to an earlier recognizer and continue when it
    completes), or, for the end of the input only, `end -> reject`.
  * A string `"true" -> word_end` is a sequence: one symbol at a time.
  * Every symbol a state names no transition for is rejected: there is no
    other kind of rejection, no regular expression, no priority, no
    longest match and no action.

== WHAT THE COMPILER PROVES
  * Determinism: the transitions of a state claim disjoint symbols
    (error.dfa-ambiguous-transition).
  * Defined rejection: every value -- outside the alphabet too -- is taken
    by exactly one transition or rejected, with std::dfa::UnrecognizedInput
    {recognizer, state, symbol} (the end: UnexpectedEnd {recognizer,
    state}).
  * Explicit acceptance and progress: a recognizer completes only through
    an `accept`; every transition takes its symbol, completes, or calls a
    recognizer that takes it.
  * Finite control: only earlier recognizers are called
    (error.dfa-recursive-call), so a recognizer's state is a state number
    and a bounded call chain.
  * Bounded lookahead: `accept before N` never hands back more symbols
    than the recognizer was given, and a token keeps at least one symbol
    unless no symbol was given at all (error.dfa-unread-beyond-input); every
    recognizer has a static largest unread count, the buffer a driver
    needs.
  * Integrity: no unreachable state, no state that never accepts, no call
    that can only reject, no state that completes before a symbol without
    saying what the end means.

== WHAT IT DOES NOT PROVE
A well-formed recognizer can recognize the wrong language: the checks are
about the declaration, not about agreement with a grammar someone else
wrote. That is what conformance corpora and differential tests are for.
Nothing about the code that drives a recognizer (its buffer, its limits)
is proved either.

== EXECUTION
A recognizer is an ordinary coroutine: eager start, typed resumes, a
stable final result or failure, affine handle, deep yields through calls.
Nothing new runs: the transition table is one internal call the native
backend compiles to a jump table, an interval search or a table load, and
coroutine fusion (COROUTINE-FUSION-MILESTONE-1.md) compiles a driver that
keeps its recognizers' handles into one loop without coroutine objects.

@example accept
import std::dfa
import std::coroutine

class digit = '0'..'9'

dfa number over ascii:
    start:
        digit -> integer
    integer:
        digit -> integer
        ',' -> accept before
        end -> accept

fn unread(first: int, next: int) -> int errors std::dfa::UnrecognizedInput, std::dfa::UnexpectedEnd:
    coroutine {step} = number(first)
    step(std::dfa::Symbol {code: next})
    step(std::dfa::Symbol {code: 44})

unread(49, 50):
    on std::dfa::UnrecognizedInput:
        -1
    on std::dfa::UnexpectedEnd:
        -2
@caption "12," is the number 12 and one symbol after it
@value 1
@end

@topic error.dfa-ambiguous-transition
title: DFA-AMBIGUOUS-TRANSITION: two transitions of one state take a symbol
summary: A recognizer takes at most one transition per symbol: two transitions of a state whose symbol sets overlap are rejected at the later one, with the symbols they share.
index: errors
alias: transitions overlap; nondeterministic transition
keywords: dfa, determinism, overlap, ambiguous, class, range
related: concept.recognizers
docs: DFA-COROUTINE-MILESTONE-2.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC DFA-AMBIGUOUS-TRANSITION

== MEANING
The order of a state's transitions means nothing: the compiler is free to
test them in any order because it proves no symbol is claimed twice. An
overlap -- often a class that already holds a symbol written separately
-- would leave the choice to the order.

== WHAT TO DO
Remove the shared symbols from one side (`'1'..'9'` instead of a class of
every digit), or write `class nonzero = digit except '0'`.

@example reject
import std::dfa

dfa number over ascii:
    start:
        '0' -> zero
        '0'..'9' -> digits
    zero:
        end -> accept
    digits:
        end -> accept

0
@caption '0' is claimed twice
@error CORE SEMANTIC DFA-AMBIGUOUS-TRANSITION
@at 6:9
@end

@topic error.dfa-unknown-state
title: DFA-UNKNOWN-STATE: a transition names no state of the recognizer
summary: A transition's target, or a call's `then` state, names no state the recognizer declares.
index: errors
alias: unknown state
keywords: dfa, state, typo, target
related: concept.recognizers
docs: DFA-COROUTINE-MILESTONE-2.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC DFA-UNKNOWN-STATE

== MEANING
States are a recognizer's own names, declared by `NAME:` lines in its
body; a transition may name a state declared after it.

== WHAT TO DO
Fix the spelling, or declare the state.

@example reject
import std::dfa

dfa d over ascii:
    start:
        'a' -> nowhere

0
@caption nowhere is no state of d
@error CORE SEMANTIC DFA-UNKNOWN-STATE
@at 5:16
@end

@topic error.dfa-unknown-class
title: DFA-UNKNOWN-CLASS: a symbol item names no class
summary: A name in a transition or a class declaration names no class of the recognizer, of its file or of the imported namespace it qualifies, or a class of the file declared after it.
index: errors
alias: unknown class
keywords: dfa, class, typo, declared later, qualified class
related: concept.recognizers error.missing-import
docs: DFA-COROUTINE-MILESTONE-2.md
code: CORE SEMANTIC DFA-UNKNOWN-CLASS

== MEANING
A class is used after it is declared, as every binding is. A bare name is
a class of the recognizer (declared first in its body) or of the file; a
qualified one, `ns::digit`, a class of module ns (after `import ns`).

== WHAT TO DO
Declare the class above its first use, or qualify it with the namespace
that declares it.

@example reject
import std::dfa

dfa d over ascii:
    start:
        digit -> start
        end -> accept

0
@caption No class digit is declared
@error CORE SEMANTIC DFA-UNKNOWN-CLASS
@at 5:9
@end

@topic error.dfa-unknown-dfa
title: DFA-UNKNOWN-DFA: a call names no earlier recognizer
summary: `call DFA then ...` names a recognizer that is not declared, or one declared after the caller: a recognizer calls only recognizers declared before it (or in an imported module), so no call can recur.
index: errors
